.TH MD 2
.SH NAME
mdparse, mdedit, mdfree, mdroot, mddown, mdnext, mdup, mdseek, mdcurcopy, mdcurfree, mddef, mdentity \- parse Markdown into a tree over its source
.SH SYNOPSIS
.ta \w'Md*\ \ \ 'u
.B #include <u.h>
.br
.B #include <libc.h>
.br
.B #include <md.h>
.PP
.B
Md*	mdparse(char *s, ulong n, int ext)
.PP
.B
Md*	mdedit(Md *m, char *s, ulong n, ulong q0, ulong q1, ulong nq1)
.PP
.B
void	mdfree(Md *m)
.PP
.B
void	mdroot(Md *m, Mdcur *c)
.PP
.B
int	mddown(Mdcur *c)
.PP
.B
int	mdnext(Mdcur *c)
.PP
.B
int	mdup(Mdcur *c)
.PP
.B
int	mdseek(Mdcur *c, ulong q)
.PP
.B
void	mdcurcopy(Mdcur *to, Mdcur *from)
.PP
.B
void	mdcurfree(Mdcur *c)
.PP
.B
int	mddef(Mdcur *link, Mdcur *def)
.PP
.B
int	mdentity(char *s, int n, Rune *r)
.PP
.B
void*	emalloc(ulong n)
.PP
.B
void*	erealloc(void *p, ulong n)
.SH DESCRIPTION
These routines parse CommonMark 0.31.2, with any of four of GitHub's extensions,
into a tree of nodes over the source.
Each node, a run of emphasis delimiters as much as a paragraph,
has the byte offsets of its start and end in the source and the line it starts on.
After an edit,
.I mdedit
parses again only near the edit and shares the rest of the old tree.
.PP
.I Mdparse
parses the
.I n
bytes of UTF-8 at
.I s
and returns a snapshot,
.IR Md .
.I Ext
is a mask of the extensions to recognize:
.B Mdxtable
for tables,
.B Mdxstrike
for strikethrough with one or two tildes,
.B Mdxauto
for links made from bare
.BR www. ,
.BR http:// ,
.B https://
and
.B ftp://
addresses and email addresses,
and
.B Mdxtask
for task list items;
.B Mdgfm
is all four, and 0 is CommonMark alone.
The snapshot holds
.I s
but does not copy it; the text must not change while the snapshot is in use.
Its
.B seq
counts the edits since
.IR mdparse .
.PP
.I Mdedit
returns a new snapshot for text that differs from
.IR m 's
in one place:
the old bytes from
.I q0
to
.I q1
are the new bytes from
.I q0
to
.IR nq1 ,
and the new text is the
.I n
bytes at
.IR s .
The result is the tree
.I mdparse
would give for the new text, node for node.
The two snapshots share the subtrees the edit did not reach.
.I Mdedit
reads only the new text, so the caller may change one buffer in place before the call;
the old snapshot then stays valid as a tree but not as text, and the caller may free it at any time after.
The new snapshot's
.B r0
and
.B r1
are the bytes of the new text that the parser read again.
Blocks elsewhere may have had their inlines parsed again too,
when the edit defines a link label they use or removes its last definition.
.I Mdfree
frees a snapshot, not its text.
.PP
A node is an
.IR Mdnode :
.IP
.EX
struct Mdnode
{
	int	type;
	int	flags;
	int	aux;
	int	c;
	int	w;
	ulong	q0;
	ulong	q1;
	ulong	line;
};
.EE
.PP
.I Q0
and
.I q1
are its start and end, in bytes, and
.I line
is the number of line endings before
.IR q0 .
A node's range holds its children's ranges, and children are in the order of their starts.
The types of blocks are
.BR Mddoc ,
.BR Mdquote ,
.BR Mdlist ,
.BR Mditem ,
.BR Mdpara ,
.BR Mdhead ,
.B Mdcode
(indented or fenced),
.B Mdhtml
(a block of raw HTML),
.BR Mdrule ,
.B Mddef
(a link reference definition),
.BR Mdtable ,
.B Mdrow
and
.BR Mdcell .
The types of inlines are
.BR Mdtext ,
.B Mdesc
(a backslash and the character it escapes),
.B Mdent
(an entity or numeric character reference),
.BR Mdsoft ,
.BR Mdhard ,
.BR Mdcodespan ,
.BR Mdemph ,
.BR Mdstrong ,
.BR Mdstrike ,
.BR Mdlink ,
.BR Mdimage ,
.B Mdauto
(an autolink)
and
.B Mdraw
(raw HTML).
An
.B Mdtext
node's bytes are always the text it stands for.
.PP
The rest of a node's syntax is in nodes of its own.
An
.B Mdmark
node is markup, and its
.I aux
is the type of the node it marks:
each run of delimiters of emphasis, strong emphasis, strikethrough and code spans,
the brackets and parentheses of links and images,
the
.B #
runs and setext underline of a heading,
the fences of a code block,
a list item's marker,
a table's pipes and delimiter row,
and every
.B >
of a block quote, on every line.
A mark is a child of the innermost node whose range holds it,
so a
.B >
at the start of a line in a quoted paragraph is among that paragraph's inlines.
.B Mddest
and
.B Mdtitle
are a link's or a definition's destination and title,
.B Mdlabel
is a reference's label,
.B Mdinfo
is a fenced code block's info string, and
.B Mdtask
is a task item's
.B [ ]
or
.BR [x] .
Their children are
.BR Mdtext ,
.BR Mdesc ,
.BR Mdent ,
and
.B Mdsoft
nodes.
A code block or block of HTML has an
.B Mdtext
child for each line, whose
.I aux
is the number of spaces a partly used tab adds before it.
A code span, raw HTML and a title have an
.B Mdtext
child for each line's part, with
.B Mdsoft
nodes between.
.PP
The flags are
.B Mdordered
and
.B Mdtight
for lists and items,
.B Mdfenced
for code blocks,
.B Mdsetext
for headings,
.B Mdheader
for a table's first row,
.B Mdfill
for a cell a short row lacked,
.B Mdtodo
and
.B Mdchecked
for task items,
.BR Mdinline ,
.BR Mdfull ,
.B Mdcollapsed
and
.B Mdshortcut
for the four kinds of links and images,
and
.BR Mdemail ,
.BR Mdwww ,
and
.B Mdbare
for autolinks.
Higher bits are the library's own.
.I Aux
is a heading's level, an ordered list's start, a cell's alignment
.RB ( l ,
.BR c ,
.B r
or 0), an HTML block's kind (1 to 7),
and a fenced code block's indentation.
.I C
is a list's or item's bullet or delimiter, and a fence's character;
.I w
is an item's content column, a fence's length, and a code span's run of backticks.
.PP
A cursor,
.IR Mdcur ,
walks a snapshot.
Its
.I Mdnode
is the node it points to, and its other fields belong to the library.
.I Mdroot
puts
.I c
at the document.
.IR Mddown ,
.I mdnext
and
.I mdup
move to the first child, the next sibling, and the parent,
and return 0, without moving, when no such node exists.
.I Mdseek
moves to the deepest node whose range holds byte
.I q
and returns its depth.
.I Mdcurcopy
makes
.I to
a cursor at the same node as
.IR from ,
and
.I mdcurfree
frees a cursor's memory.
The tree holds no parent pointers; a cursor keeps the path it took.
.PP
.I Mddef
puts
.I def
at the definition a reference link or image at
.I link
uses, the first in the document with its label,
and returns 0 if
.I link
is not one.
.I Mdentity
decodes the reference of
.I n
bytes at
.IR s ,
an
.B Mdent
node's, into
.I r
(one or two runes, with a zero after one) and returns its length, or 0 if the bytes are not a reference.
.PP
The library allocates with the program's
.I emalloc
and
.IR erealloc ,
which do not return when memory runs out;
.I emalloc
zeroes what it returns.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print each heading's text:
.IP
.EX
Md *m;
Mdcur c;
int d;

m = mdparse(s, n, Mdgfm);
memset(&c, 0, sizeof c);
mdroot(m, &c);
for(d = 0;;){
	if(c.type == Mdhead)
		print("%.*s\en", utfnlen(s+c.q0, c.q1-c.q0), s+c.q0);
	if(mddown(&c)){
		d++;
		continue;
	}
	while(!mdnext(&c))
		if(d-- == 0 || !mdup(&c))
			goto done;
}
done:
mdcurfree(&c);
.EE
